; Human (Villager) — AnimalDefinition data entry (spec.md -> Data Schemas).
; Roster row: roster.md -> Already-Defined Roster, the "Human (Villager)" row.
; Asset: Quaternius standalone character glb (Adventurer), CC0 1.0 Universal — see
;   project/attribution/sources/quaternius_poly_pizza_characters.tres.
;
; VILLAGERS ARE JUST ANOTHER SPECIES (gdd.md -> Design Pillars). There is no people schema,
; no separate economy, and no special case anywhere in the simulation: this file is loaded
; by the same SpeciesRoster loader as fox.tres and rabbit.tres, and a villager moving in is
; the same event as any other move-in. That is the whole point — row 4's USP proof is that
; the House-plus-farm habitat produces a resident through the ordinary qualification path.
;
; DECIDED VALUES, transcribed from roster.md's Already-Defined row — not proposed here:
;   habitat_needs = ["house", "cultivated"]   personality = Bold   avoids = (none)
;   farm_tolerant = true                      tiles_per_individual = 1
; roster.md's rationale for the divisor: "Human's divisor is 1 because the House is the
; scarce need and the floor House is a single tile; the 2x2 form supports up to four
; families, given fields to match."
;
; NO `avoids` ENTRY, and that closes a gate rather than leaving one open: roster.md's
; structural predation check runs only when a species gains an avoids entry, so Human's
; predation risk is closed by data, not by copy.
;
; PLACEHOLDER — the human owns this: `scout_radius = 8`. spec.md gives the band ~8-12
; (Open Question #20) and states no Human value. 8 is proposed at the band's tight end
; because a villager's two needs are a House and the field beside it, and gdd.md notes the
; radius "is the mechanism that makes the land-allocation tradeoff bite" (#23) — a compact
; village is the shape a tight radius produces. Listed under Proposals in the build report.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; OPEN QUESTION #31 — COPY WRITTEN AND SOURCE-VERIFIED 2026-07-28. AWAITING STEP-8 SIGN-OFF.
; SECOND-SOURCE PASS 2026-07-28: card was single-sourced (ADW only) because Nat Geo Kids'
; domain was unreachable. Domain is back and education.nationalgeographic.org was added to
; spec.md's approved set. Result: PARTIALLY CORROBORATED — clause 2's date was OVER-PRECISE
; and clause 3's causal framing was HARDER THAN THE SOURCES. Both were corrected; the
; shipped string CHANGED. See "SOURCES REACHED" and "PER-CLAUSE SOURCING" below, which are
; rewritten to the double-sourced form; every clause now carries a non-ADW source.
;
; `fact_text` below is content-writer's option A, written at Content Pipeline step 5
; against spec.md -> Fact-Card Content Checklist. The register was never in question
; (roster.md -> Villagers): the villager's move-in card is a REAL fact card asserting a
; real-world fact, not flavor, because the two-register rule does not bend for the one
; species the player happens to be.
;
; SOURCES REACHED (all fetched and quoted, none recalled):
;   [ADW]  https://animaldiversity.org/accounts/Homo_sapiens/
;   [NGE-A] Nat Geo Society Education, "The Development of Agriculture"
;          https://education.nationalgeographic.org/resource/development-agriculture/
;   [NGE-B] "Hunter-Gatherer Culture"
;          https://education.nationalgeographic.org/resource/hunter-gatherer-culture/
;   [NGE-C] "Agricultural Communities"
;          https://education.nationalgeographic.org/resource/resource-library-agricultural-communities/
;   [NGE-D] "Early Agricultural Communities"
;          https://education.nationalgeographic.org/resource/early-agricultural-communities/
; NAT GEO KIDS is reachable again (verified: an article loads) but carries NO human-origins
; or agriculture-emergence page. Its nearest article, Ancient Egypt, is one region at
; "as early as 6000 B.C." — far too local and too late to source a worldwide claim. Recorded
; so nobody re-runs this search: NGK is not a source for this card. (Same lesson as spec.md's
; Smithsonian-has-no-red-fox-page note — do not assume a site covers a topic.)
;
; PER-CLAUSE SOURCING — every clause now carries a NON-ADW source.
;   "people moved from place to place instead of staying in one home"        CORROBORATED
;     <- [ADW] "Ancient humans were nomadic hunter gatherers..." (the NOMADIC half only)
;     <- [NGE-A] "They switched from nomadic hunter-gatherer lifestyles to permanent
;         settlements and farming." + "Traditional hunter-gatherer lifestyles, followed by
;         humans since their evolution, were swept aside"
;     <- [NGE-B] "most hunter-gatherers were nomadic"
;      NOTE the "most" hedge in NGE-B. NGE-A states it unhedged and universally, and the
;      card's "Long ago" is a time anchor, not an all-people quantifier, so the clause
;      stands as written. Flagged for the human anyway — see the build report.
;
;   "About 10,000 to 12,000 years ago they learned to grow their own food"   REWRITTEN
;     WAS: "About 10,000 years ago". The sources DISAGREE, and the disagreement runs
;     straight through the publication the human added to carry exactly this material:
;       [ADW]   "the development of agriculture approximately 10,000 years ago"
;       [NGE-C] "Agricultural communities developed approximately 10,000 years ago"
;       [NGE-A] "The development of agricultural about 12,000 years ago changed the way
;                humans lived." + "Taking root around 12,000 years ago"
;       [NGE-B] "the Neolithic Revolution about 12,000 years ago, when agricultural
;                practices were first developed"
;       [NGE-D] "10,000 to 15,000 years ago, during the Neolithic Age"
;     A flat "about 10,000" sat at the extreme low edge of the range and was contradicted
;     by the two MOST ON-POINT pages. The range form is true under every source fetched.
;     This is the over-precision trap: the number read as settled and was not.
;
;   "and little by little they settled down in one place"                    REWRITTEN
;     WAS: "and that is when people began to settle down in one place". The direction of
;     causation holds, but "that is when" pins a gradual process to a moment, which the
;     sources explicitly refuse:
;       [NGE-C] "By domesticating species, many groups of people were able to build settled
;                communities and transition from a nomadic hunter-gatherer lifestyle"
;                — the strongest causal support, and note "many groups", not all people
;       [NGE-A] "Though the transition from wild harvesting was gradual" — an explicit
;                gradualness hedge, in the source's own voice
;       [NGE-A] "the switch from a nomadic to a settled way of life is MARKED BY the
;                appearance of early Neolithic villages" — a marker, not a cause
;       [NGE-B] "some groups abandoned hunter-gatherer practices to establish permanent
;                settlements"
;       [ADW]   "Agriculture ultimately led to increases in regional human populations and
;                concentration in urban centers..." — note ADW's own "ultimately"
;     "little by little" carries the gradualness in words a six-year-old already owns, and
;     softens the all-people reading at the same time. The card no longer claims a date on
;     which people settled; it claims a slow change that growing food made possible.
;
; HABITAT-KEYED, exactly as the fox card keys off cover: growing food IS the `cultivated`
; need and settling in one place IS the `house` need, so the card explains in real-world
; terms why this species' habitat is a house with a field beside it.
;
; BANNED VOCABULARY, recorded so it is never re-shown: ADW's sentence reads "nomadic hunter
; gatherers". The copy rests on the CONCLUSION — people moved from place to place — and can
; never show that work. Same rule the Rabbit warren fact runs under (rabbit.tres).
;
; ROSTER-WIDE TERMINOLOGY CHECK (the D-19 "kits"/"kittens" lesson: read other species'
; SHIPPED COPY, not just the sources). "town" and "village" were drafted and CUT: this
; entry's `display_name` is "Villager" and the HUD counter is "Village Population", so a
; real-world claim carrying those words reads as a claim about the game world — precisely
; what the two-register rule forbids. "family", "home", "den", "warren" as used in
; fox.tres, rabbit.tres and the news-report pools are untouched by this card.
;
; RE-RUN 2026-07-28 for the rewritten clauses. One NEAR-MISS found and cleared, recorded so
; it is not re-litigated: "SETTLED" is already shipped player-facing copy in the GAME
; register — docs/content/fox-news-report-pool.md ("a fox family has settled in"),
; rabbit-news-report-pool.md ("a group of rabbits has settled in"), and displacement-copy.md
; — where it means "moved into your world". This card uses it in the FACT register. It
; clears because, unlike "town"/"village", "settled" is not a HUD label but ordinary
; narrative verb, and the clause is anchored by "Long ago" and a date, which fixes the
; register before the verb arrives. Had the card said "the first people settled in", it
; would NOT have cleared. New words "little by little" and "12,000" collide with nothing.
; CONSISTENCY GAINED: displacement-copy.md's Human lines say the farm family needs "better
; soil to grow in" and "room to grow"; this card says people "learned to grow their own
; food". Same verb, same `cultivated` need, across both registers — reinforcing, not
; colliding, and worth keeping if either string is ever revised.
;
; PREDATION / GRAPH CHECK: `avoids` is empty, so the structural predation check stays closed
; by data (see above). The copy names no other species, no diet, and no danger.
;
; NOTE FOR QA — ROUND 2, 2026-07-28. THE SHIPPED STRING CHANGED AGAIN. `test_human_schema.gd`
; and `test_fact_card.gd` now pin the previous wording with EXACT EQUALITY, so both go red
; on this edit. That is intended and it is qa-engineer's to re-point, not content-writer's.
; The one and only correct value is the `fact_text` literal at the bottom of this file —
; copy it from there, do not retype it. Two characters are easy to lose: the comma-free
; "10,000 to 12,000" range and "little by little".
; (Round 1, for the record: this file's copy first replaced a placeholder, invalidating
; `begins_with PLACEHOLDER_MARKER`, `contains "#31"`, validate()-returns-exactly-one-problem,
; and `_check_human_placeholder_renders_as_authored()`. Pin it the way `test_fox_schema.gd`
; pins the fox card: exact-string equality, a not-a-placeholder check, a banned-word sweep,
; and validate() clean.)
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; NEWS_REPORTS, added 2026-08-09 (Tier 1 row 12, closing the schema gap tier1-status.md named
; a THIRD time over — fox, then rabbit, then human). Nine lines from
; docs/content/human-news-report-pool.md's Discovery/hint and Ambient/flavor sub-pools,
; checklist-passed 2026-08-06 reusing this very fact card's already-verified habitat-need
; clauses (spec.md:78 — News Report content reuses the fact-card pipeline, not a second
; sourcing pass). Move-in is not included here — it already has its own channel (this fact
; card) — and Avoidance-relocation / Symmetric-avoids are explicitly N/A for this species
; (`avoids` is empty; see the pool file's own "Avoids: does not apply" section).
;
; MODEL_SCENES GROWN TO 5 VARIANTS (content-variety pass, human-confirmed): a new villager
; randomly gets one of Adventurer/Man/Woman/Hoodie/Punk (equal weight, no ordering
; preference — same D-42 precedent forest.tres's 3-tree pool uses), stably picked per
; resident by AnimalDefinition.pick_variant(), keyed on the resident's index within its
; home site's `residents` array. All 5 source scenes were already imported and wrapped
; before this pass (assets/animals/human_{man,woman,hoodie,punk}/*.tscn) — no new asset
; import, only wiring.
;
; MODEL_SCENES GROWN 5 -> 18 (2026-08-29, Content Pipeline step 4 — model_scenes wiring ONLY;
; no other AnimalDefinition field is touched by this pass, and none needs to be: Human is a
; signed-off floor species (D-28) whose habitat_needs, personality, avoids, farm_tolerant and
; the three capacity constants are settled). 13 character wrappers appended, all imported,
; wrapped, tested and attributed by tech-art before this pass. Index 0 (Adventurer) stays
; index 0, unchanged. Variant selection is still AnimalDefinition.pick_variant(), stable per
; resident, equal weight, no ordering preference (D-42 precedent) — a bigger pool, same rule.
;
; NO NEW FACT CARD, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. These are LOOK VARIANTS of an item whose copy
; already exists: `fact_text_pool` below is unchanged and still carries the source-verified,
; double-sourced card documented at length above. A variant does not get its own fact card,
; so Content Pipeline step 5 is not reopened by this pass.
;
; BASE AND `Smooth_` SETS ARE BOTH WIRED, ON PURPOSE — THIS IS A TEMPORARY COMPARISON STATE.
; Human ruling (2026-08-29): "import both, decide in-engine." The two sets are the same
; characters authored at different mesh smoothing, and which set reads better under this
; game's fixed ~45 degree camera is a look call that cannot be made from a file listing. Both
; are therefore in the pool at once so they can be seen side by side in a running game. THE
; LOSING SET GETS PULLED once the human decides; until then, do not "deduplicate" this array
; — the apparent duplication IS the experiment. Pulling a set afterwards is a model_scenes
; edit plus removal of the corresponding ext_resource lines; the wrappers and attribution
; entries stay on disk either way.
;
;   base   (5, incl. index 0):  Adventurer, Man, AnimatedWoman, HoodieCharacter, Punk
;   base   (male, added):       MaleLongSleeve, MaleShirt
;   base   (female, added):     FemaleAlternative, FemaleCasual, FemaleDress, FemaleTankTop
;   Smooth (male, added):       SmoothMaleCasual, SmoothMaleLongSleeve, SmoothMaleShirt
;   Smooth (female, added):     SmoothFemaleAlternative, SmoothFemaleCasual, SmoothFemaleDress,
;                               SmoothFemaleTankTop
;
; HELD OUT ON PURPOSE — Male_Suit and Smooth_Male_Suit. Their absence is intentional, not a
; missed import: art.md's palette rule excludes them at Kd 0.0185, i.e. effectively pure
; black, which does not sit in this game's palette. Do not add them back without a human
; ruling on that rule.
;
; ASYMMETRY WORTH SEEING, raised not decided: the base set has no `human_male_casual` on disk
; while the Smooth set does have SmoothMaleCasual, so the two sets are 6-vs-7 rather than a
; clean pairing. That is a property of the source pack as imported, not of this wiring, but it
; means the in-engine comparison is not perfectly like-for-like. FLAG FOR HUMAN SIGN-OFF.
[gd_resource type="Resource" script_class="AnimalDefinition" load_steps=20 format=3]

